.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# The store is data, not source, and only one part of it can leave Wirtek.
#
# `store/raw/` holds each payload as the publisher served it. It is the irreplaceable part:
# the DuckDB file rebuilds from it, and it does not rebuild from the network, because an API
# serves today and not 2026-09-17. The payloads of the 5 licensed sources are therefore in
# this repository. They make the published numbers reproducible.
#
# 2 sources must never reach a commit: the Denmark series from Energinet, and the Great
# Britain series from NESO. `LICENSE-DATA.md`, section "The two series you must not
# redistribute yet", gives the reason. `sources/sources.json` marks both
# `licence_status: not recorded`, because nobody read the reuse terms of either publisher.
# Both series are free to read, and that says nothing about the right to republish them.
# Until somebody reads those terms and records them, both series stay inside Wirtek.
#
# A history keeps a file after a delete, so 1 payload in 1 commit costs a history rewrite.
# Never use `git add -f` on either path, and read `git status --ignored` before each
# `git add`.
#
# The rules below deny by default. `store/*` and `store/raw/*` ignore the whole store, and 1
# negation line per licensed source allows that source back. A source added tomorrow is
# ignored until somebody adds its line. Git does not add a file back if an ignored directory
# holds it, so the rules allow each level in turn: first `store/raw/`, then the source
# directory. `store/atlas.duckdb`, `store/log/`, `store/tmp/` and every new file at the top
# of `store/` stay ignored.
#
# To publish a new source: record its terms in `sources/sources.json` and in `NOTICE.md`,
# then add 1 `!store/raw/<source>/` line to the list below.
store/*
!store/raw/
store/raw/*
!store/raw/energy-charts-co2eq/
!store/raw/energy-charts-co2eq-snap/
!store/raw/rte-eco2mix-odre/
!store/raw/elia-co2-production/
!store/raw/elia-co2-consumption/

# The 2 blocked sources, named again after the negations. `store/raw/*` above already ignores
# them, and these 3 lines make the decision visible at the point of the rule. They also hold
# if somebody later widens a negation above them. A rule that comes later wins, so keep these
# 3 lines last in this block and add no negation below them.
store/raw/energinet-eds/
store/raw/neso-carbon-intensity/
store/raw/neso-carbon-intensity-snap/

# `portal/data/` is the export of the same series, the 2 blocked ones included, and it
# rebuilds from the store in minutes.
portal/data/

# The design mock of 2026-09-17. The working portal in `portal/` superseded it on
# 2026-09-18. Every number in it is generated, and a generated number that sits in a
# repository beside a measured one is a question waiting to be asked. It also carried a
# second copy of the 5 Wirtek brand images. It stays on disk as a record of the design,
# and it is not part of the deliverable.
portal/mock/

# The store does not have to be at `store/`. `atlas/config.py:21` reads the location from
# the ATLAS_STORE environment variable, so 1 variable can put a 359 MiB DuckDB file at a
# path inside this repository that the rule above does not match. DuckDB also rewrites
# large parts of the file on each run, so each commit of it would add a near-full copy.
*.duckdb
*.duckdb.wal
*.duckdb.tmp

# Artefacts of a tool run. None of them is source. All 3 appeared in the root of the
# delivered copy: a Cygwin crash dump, and the caches of pytest and pytest-benchmark.
# `.pytest_cache/` also holds a `lastfailed` record, and pytest is not the supported
# test runner. See requirements.txt.
#
# `.pytest_cache/v/cache/nodeids` and `lastfailed` also hold the Windows account name of
# a developer, inside a temporary path. That is the only place in the tree where a person's
# name is in a file. Do not relax these 3 lines.
.pytest_cache/
.benchmarks/
*.stackdump

# Nothing below matches a file in the tree today. Each line is insurance against the next
# accident, and a first commit is the only cheap moment to add it. A history keeps a file
# after a delete, so a credential that arrives once is permanent.

# Credentials and key material. These patterns are deliberately wide, because the cost of a
# wrong exclusion is that a file does not appear in `git status`, and the cost of a wrong
# inclusion is a leaked key. `SOURCES.md` §8 plans an ENTSO-E security token, so a file with
# `token` in its name is a real arrival and not a hypothetical one. `atlas/config.py:103`
# reads ATLAS_CA_BUNDLE, so a proxy CA file is one too.
# To commit a file that one of these patterns catches, add a `!` line below it.
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.crt
*.cer
*.jks
*.keystore
.netrc
_netrc
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
*token*
*secret*
*credential*

# Machine-local tool state. The agent tools write into the working directory, and their
# files hold absolute paths and permission lists of 1 machine. `.claude/settings.json` is
# shared project configuration, so the negation keeps it available if the team wants it.
.claude/*
!.claude/settings.json
.playwright-mcp/

# Editor and IDE state. Personal, not project.
.vscode/
.idea/
*.code-workspace
*.swp
*.swo

# Files that an operating system writes beside yours.
.DS_Store
._*
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Scratch, backup and merge leftovers. A `.orig` or a `.rej` file is a half-finished merge,
# and a `.log` file records absolute paths and request URLs.
*.tmp
*.temp
*.bak
*.orig
*.rej
*~
*.log

# Python build and analysis output. This project has no build step today. If one arrives,
# its output is generated and does not belong in the history.
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
.tox/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# The portal has no bundler and no `package.json`, and that is a strength. If one arrives,
# its dependency tree still does not belong here.
node_modules/
